{"task":"triaj concerns: bryce-m5.local","goals":"Repo-level concerns with no commit to hang on","status":"Waiting","concerns_owner":"bryce-m5.local","standalone_concerns":[{"id":"cn-b3814414bca7","text":"persite mode gives every site the same Caddy admin port, so the second site cannot start","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"complete","at":"2026-09-08T11:20:51.761Z","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","related":[],"resolved_at":"2026-09-08T11:37:49.827Z","resolved_by":"476ad4b44be9afecbeaabb1ca92c8236a379ab27","resolved_by_actor":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad"},{"id":"cn-c00f8ddf0002","text":"Per-site mode has no way to run a site as a root daemon, so port 443 is unreachable by design\n\npersite runs each site as a per-user LaunchAgent, and on macOS an unprivileged process cannot bind a specific address on a port below 1024. So a persite site can serve 443 to the whole network, or not serve 443 at all — it cannot serve 443 to loopback. The bare https://<site>.<tld> URL is therefore unavailable in the mode, with no hint from the tool that this is why. Restoring it for matsu.ldev needed a hand-written LaunchDaemon plist and a swap script kept outside the repo in ~/.config/ldev/matsu-443. If that is worth supporting, ldev new (or an ldev daemon <site> subcommand) could emit the daemon plist alongside the Caddyfile and file the one privileged step, instead of each site being rebuilt by hand.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T10:59:46.936Z","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","related":[]},{"id":"cn-01207326911e","text":"install.sh does not tidy up the mode it is replacing, so two topologies end up live at once\n\nObserved 2026-09-09 on this machine. The box was set up in persite mode; a later run of install.sh chose auto. That run rewrote ~/.config/ldev/config to MODE=auto and installed /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.ldev.caddy.plist, but left every per-site Caddy running and every per-site Caddyfile in place. Both topologies were then live: the auto daemon held port 80, a per-site daemon held 443, and https://ldev/ answered from neither — it returned 000 ten times out of ten, because the process on 443 has no site matching Host: ldev. Nothing warned, and nothing failed loudly; the install reported success. An install that changes MODE should either refuse while the other mode is running and say what is running, or take the other mode down as part of switching. Related: the config it writes then describes a machine that does not exist, which is the second concern filed alongside this one.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"complete","at":"2026-09-09T15:29:22.402Z","by":"claude:eb23749b-b71d-44b3-ab30-5c8053a65845","related":[],"resolved_at":"2026-09-10T02:16:36.699Z","resolved_by":"f6723e2ee9a1b99674200e7fc0192bdd75dd7a69","resolved_by_actor":"claude:3e8f56a2-7837-42a5-adfb-4e072f3876ee"},{"id":"cn-225398076817","text":"ldev doctor trusts MODE from config rather than what is actually serving\n\nConsequence of the mode-switch gap. With MODE=auto written into ~/.config/ldev/config while the machine was really per-site, doctor took the auto branch and reported a failure for https://ldev/ while all three real sites were serving 200. The check was correct about the URL it probed and wrong about the machine. Two candidate fixes: cross-check MODE against what is on disk (per-site Caddyfiles under $SITES, /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.ldev.caddy.plist) and say so when they disagree, rather than silently believing the file; and/or have doctor report the mode it INFERRED next to the mode configured. Flipping MODE back to persite by hand made doctor green with no other change, which is the evidence that the machine was fine and only the config was wrong.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T15:29:22.564Z","by":"claude:eb23749b-b71d-44b3-ab30-5c8053a65845","related":[]},{"id":"cn-24d145832c74","text":"The auto daemon cannot install its own CA, and only says so in a log nobody reads\n\nFrom ~/Library/Logs/ldev/caddy.err.log while com.ldev.caddy was running: {\"level\":\"error\",\"logger\":\"pki.ca.local\",\"msg\":\"failed to install root certificate\",\"error\":\"failed to execute sudo: exit status 1\"}. The daemon carries on and serves, so nothing surfaces it — every auto-mode HTTPS site would show a certificate warning and the operator would have no pointer to the cause. install.sh already knows it needs sudo for the trust step and asks for it interactively; a daemon that discovers the same need at runtime cannot ask, so it should fail the check visibly (doctor) rather than log and continue.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T15:29:22.738Z","by":"claude:eb23749b-b71d-44b3-ab30-5c8053a65845","related":[]},{"id":"cn-000bb4567374","text":"Removing a daemon leaves a disabled record that makes the NEXT install fail with an unreadable error\n\nlaunchd keeps a persistent per-label disabled record in the system domain that outlives both 'launchctl bootout' and deleting the plist. A later 'launchctl bootstrap system <plist>' for that label then fails with 'Bootstrap failed: 5: Input/output error' — a message naming neither the label nor the word disabled, so it reads as a broken plist. Cost me two failed installs of a hand-written daemon before 'launchctl print-disabled system' showed the label as disabled; running 'launchctl enable system/<label>' BEFORE bootstrap fixed it outright. ldev installs and removes its own LaunchDaemon, so it will hit this the first time anyone reinstalls after an uninstall. Worth doing on both sides: enable before every bootstrap, and enable as part of removal so the label is left clean.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"complete","at":"2026-09-09T15:29:22.907Z","by":"claude:eb23749b-b71d-44b3-ab30-5c8053a65845","related":[],"resolved_at":"2026-09-10T02:16:37.022Z","resolved_by":"f6723e2ee9a1b99674200e7fc0192bdd75dd7a69","resolved_by_actor":"claude:3e8f56a2-7837-42a5-adfb-4e072f3876ee"},{"id":"cn-781fb612b500","text":"Commit 7a7f4df is genuinely broken; land the tip, never cherry-pick it\n\nOn claude/lucid-ember-7451, commit 7a7f4df (\"BROKEN: port_owner aborts the install before it asks anything\") is a real broken state, committed deliberately rather than hidden: port_owner ended in an lsof|awk pipeline, lsof exits 1 on a root-owned socket it cannot see, pipefail promoted that, and set -e killed the installer right after the banner on any machine with something on port 80. It was fixed two commits later in f6967d0. The branch tip is green (147 + 17 + 6 + 12 assertions after merging master at a9234d8), and a normal merge of this branch delivers the tip, so master is safe by that route. The hazard is only for a rebase, a cherry-pick, or a bisect that lands on 7a7f4df or e5dc95b/b3ecd50/25bb17c, several of which are mid-flight checkpoints taken because a stop hook required uncommitted work to be recorded. If this branch is ever squashed or rebased, do not pick those commits individually.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T19:26:12.563Z","by":"claude:eb23749b-b71d-44b3-ab30-5c8053a65845","related":[]},{"id":"cn-956644a3da61","text":"The machine is back on persite while the branch that removes persite is unmerged\n\nObserved 2026-09-10. A peer removed the auto daemon per Bryce (/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.ldev.caddy.plist deleted, port 80 dead, ~/.config/ldev/matsu-443/remove-auto-daemon.sh added) and restored MODE=persite, bumping ADMIN_PORT 2019 to 2021. Port 443 is now held by a root-owned daemon OUTSIDE ldev — com.bryce.caddy-matsu.plist, hand-built — which is why https://matsu.ldev/ returns 200. That is the exact workaround cn-c00f8ddf0002 describes, and the thing ldev standalone on claude/brave-cedar-fb33 (merge 5a35d53) was built to replace: a standalone site fronted on 443 by the wildcard server, managed by ldev rather than by a plist kept in a side directory. Nothing is broken right now — the branch is unmerged and matsu works — but the two states are incompatible and landing the branch on this machine means retiring com.bryce.caddy-matsu.plist and the matsu-443 directory as part of the switch, not alongside it. Related: cn-01207326911e (install.sh does not tidy up the topology it replaces) is the general form of the same hazard, and it now has a concrete instance waiting.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-10T00:45:41.010Z","by":"claude:3e8f56a2-7837-42a5-adfb-4e072f3876ee","related":[]},{"id":"cn-f69544762c00","text":"A foreign server on 443 makes every unknown host fail as ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR, and doctor calls the port healthy\n\nObserved 2026-09-10. /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.bryce.caddy-matsu.plist holds 443 and its only site block there is \"matsu.ldev, matsu-dev.ldev:8444\". https://matsu.ldev/ returns 200; https://seasonal-drops.ldev/ aborts the handshake with a TLS alert internal error, which Chrome shows as ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR, while https://seasonal-drops.ldev:8443/ returns 200. Nothing is being served on 443 for that host at all, so the browser error names TLS and points nowhere near the cause. doctor checks only that SOMETHING is listening on 443 (nc -z) and reports it green, so the layer that is actually wrong is the one that passes. It could compare what answers on 443 against what it expects: probe a hostname it knows has no site (it already builds ldev-doctor-probe.<tld> for the DNS check) and report that the wildcard fallback is not serving when the handshake fails, and/or check whether the loaded LaunchDaemon on 443 is com.ldev.caddy rather than something else. Same family as cn-225398076817, which is about believing MODE over what is serving; this is believing an open socket over what is behind it. The machine-state half is cn-956644a3da61.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"complete","at":"2026-09-10T02:03:39.735Z","by":"claude:3e8f56a2-7837-42a5-adfb-4e072f3876ee","related":[],"resolved_at":"2026-09-10T02:16:37.306Z","resolved_by":"f6723e2ee9a1b99674200e7fc0192bdd75dd7a69","resolved_by_actor":"claude:3e8f56a2-7837-42a5-adfb-4e072f3876ee"}]}
